Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 16, verse 3:

His saying, exalted is He: ﴿He created the heavens and the earth in truth; exalted is He above what they associate with Him.﴾

In this noble verse, He—majestic and exalted—made clear that He is the Creator of the heavens and the earth, and that whoever creates these great creatures is far exalted, and too great, to be worshipped alongside one who creates nothing and does not even possess anything for himself.

The verse indicates that whoever brings creation from non-existence into existence, it is not valid for there to be worshipped along with Him one who has no power over anything. For this reason, He followed His saying ﴿He created the heavens and the earth in truth﴾ [al-Nahl: 3] with His saying ﴿exalted is He above what they associate with Him﴾ [al-Nahl: 3].

He clarified this meaning in many verses:

• Such as His saying ﴿O mankind, worship your Lord who created you﴾, the verse [al-Baqarah: 21]; it indicates that the one worshipped is the Creator alone, to the exclusion of others;

• and His saying: ﴿Is then He who creates like one who does not create? Will you not be reminded?﴾ [al-Nahl: 17];

• and His saying: ﴿Or have they set up for Allah partners who created like His creation, so that the creation seemed alike to them? Say: Allah is the Creator of all things, and He is the One, the Subduer.﴾ [al-Ra'd: 16];

• and His saying: ﴿Blessed is He who sent down the Criterion upon His servant that he may be a warner to the worlds, He to whom belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth, who has not taken a son and has no partner in dominion, and who created all things and determined them precisely. Yet they have taken, besides Him, gods that create nothing, while they are themselves created, and they possess not for themselves any harm or benefit, and they possess not death or life or resurrection.﴾ [al-Furqan: 1–3];

• and His saying, exalted is He: ﴿This is the creation of Allah; so show Me what those other than Him have created. Rather, the wrongdoers are in clear error.﴾ [Luqman: 11];

• and His saying: ﴿Say: Have you considered your partners whom you invoke besides Allah? Show me what they have created from the earth, or do they have a share in the heavens?﴾, the verse [Fatir: 40];

• and His saying: ﴿Say: Have you considered what you invoke besides Allah? Show me what they have created from the earth, or do they have a share in the heavens? Bring me a book from before this, or a trace of knowledge, if you are truthful.﴾ [al-Ahqaf: 4];

• and His saying, exalted is He: ﴿Do they associate with Him that which creates nothing, while they themselves are created?﴾ [al-A'raf: 191], (p-330)

• and His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿O mankind, a parable is presented, so listen to it: Indeed, those you invoke besides Allah will never create a fly, even if they gathered together for it﴾ [al-Hajj: 73];

• and His saying: ﴿Were they created from nothing, or are they the creators? Or did they create the heavens and the earth?﴾ ... the verse [al-Tur: 35–36];

• and His saying: ﴿And those they invoke besides Allah create nothing, while they are themselves created. They are dead, not living﴾, the verse [al-Nahl: 20–21];

and other verses besides these.

These verses make clear that the one who deserves to be worshipped is the one who creates creation and brings them from non-existence into existence. As for other than Him, it is a created thing that is nurtured, in need of One who creates it and manages its affairs.

﴿It is He who sent down water from the sky for you; from it is drink, and from it are trees in which you pasture [your livestock].﴾ The discussion of what clarifies the meaning of this noble verse has already preceded in "Surat al-Hijr".

And His saying, exalted and majestic is He: ﴿And from it are trees in which you pasture [your livestock]. He causes to grow for you thereby the crops, the olives, the palm trees, the grapes, and of all the fruits. Indeed in that is a sign for a people who give thought.﴾

In this noble verse, exalted and majestic is He, He makes clear that the growth He produces by water, of the grains and fruits that people eat and of the pasturage that livestock eat, is among the greatest of His blessings upon the children of Adam, and among the clearest of His signs indicating that He alone is worthy of being worshipped. He clarified this meaning in many verses, such as:

• His saying: ﴿Have they not seen that We drive the water to the barren land and bring forth thereby crops of which their livestock and they themselves eat? Will they not then see?﴾ [As-Sajdah: 27];

• His saying: ﴿He who made the earth for you as a cradle and threaded for you therein roads and sent down from the sky water, and We brought forth thereby pairs of various plants. Eat and graze your livestock. Indeed in that are signs for people of intellect﴾ [Ta-Ha: 53, 54];

• His saying: ﴿And the earth—after that He spread it out. He extracted from it its water and its pasture. And the mountains He set firmly, as enjoyment for you and your livestock﴾ [An-Nazi'at: 30–33];

• His saying: ﴿And We sent down from the sky blessed water, and We caused to grow thereby gardens and the grain of harvest, and tall palm trees with layered clusters, as provision for the servants﴾ [Qaf: 9–11], and the verse [continues];

• His saying: ﴿Is He who created the heavens and the earth and sent down for you water from the sky, and We caused to grow thereby gardens of beauty, which you could not have grown the trees thereof? Is there a god with Allah? Rather, they are a people who deviate﴾ [An-Naml: 60];

• His saying: ﴿And We sent down from the rain-laden clouds pouring water, that We may bring forth thereby grain and vegetation, and gardens of dense growth﴾ [An-Naba': 14–16].

Verses like these are very numerous.

Two admonitions.

The first: Know that reflection upon these verses is obligatory, because it is established in the principles of jurisprudence (al-Usul, i.e., usul al-fiqh) that "the form of the command (sigha al-amr) requires obligation, unless there is evidence that diverts it from obligation." Allah, exalted and majestic is He, has commanded man to look at his food by which he lives, and to reflect upon the water that is the cause of the growth of grain: Who sent it down? Then, after the sending down of water and the watering of the earth, who is able to split the earth for the plant and bring it forth from it? Then who is able to bring the grain out of that plant? Then who is able to cause it to grow until it becomes fit for eating? ﴿Look at its fruit when it bears fruit and at its ripening...﴾ [Al-An'am: 9]. This is in His saying: ﴿So let man look at his food. That We pour down water in torrents. Then We split the earth in splitting. And We cause to grow therein grain, and grapes and herbage, and olives and palm trees, and enclosed gardens of dense trees, and fruit and grass, as enjoyment for you and your livestock﴾ [Abasa: 24–32].

Likewise, it is obligatory upon man to reflect upon the thing from which he was created, because of His saying: ﴿So let man look from what he was created﴾ [At-Tariq: 5]. The apparent meaning of the Qur'an is that reflection upon this is obligatory, and there is no evidence diverting from that.

The second admonition: Know that in these verses, from the beginning of Surat an-Nahl, He, exalted and majestic is He, pointed to the three proofs of resurrection (al-Ba'th), which we have already mentioned that the Noble Qur'an frequently uses as evidence for resurrection.

The first: the creation of the heavens and the earth, mentioned in His saying: ﴿He created the heavens and the earth with truth﴾ [At-Taghabun: 3]. The argument from this for resurrection is frequent in the Qur'an, such as His saying: ﴿Are you more difficult to create or is the sky? He constructed it; He raised its height﴾ [An-Nazi'at: 27, 28], up to His saying: ﴿as enjoyment for you and your livestock﴾ [An-Nazi'at: 33];

• and His saying: ﴿Do they not see that Allah, who created the heavens and the earth and was not fatigued by their creation, is able to give life to the dead?﴾ [Al-Ahqaf: 33];

• and His saying: ﴿The creation of the heavens and the earth is greater than the creation of mankind﴾ [Ghafir: 57], and the verse [continues];

• and His saying: ﴿Is not He who created the heavens and the earth able to create the likes of them? Yes, and He is the Creator, the All-Knowing﴾ [Ya-Sin: 81];

and other verses as previously mentioned.

The second proof: the first creation of man, mentioned in His saying: ﴿He created man from a sperm-drop﴾ [An-Nahl: 4]; because He who originated is able to restore a second time. This too is frequently used as evidence for resurrection,

• such as His saying: ﴿Say: He will give them life who produced them the first time, and He is Knowing of every creation﴾ [Ya-Sin: 79];

• and His saying: ﴿And it is He who begins creation and then repeats it, and that is easier for Him﴾ [Ar-Rum: 27], and the verse [continues];

• and His saying: ﴿O mankind, if you are in doubt about resurrection, then indeed, We created you from dust﴾ [Al-Hajj: 5];

• and His saying: ﴿Were We fatigued by the first creation? Rather, they are in confusion about a new creation﴾ [Qaf: 15];

and other verses (p-339) as previously mentioned.

The third proof: the revival of the earth after its death, mentioned here in His saying: ﴿He causes to grow for you thereby the crops, the olives, the palm trees, and the grapes﴾ [An-Nahl: 11]. The argument from this for resurrection is also frequent in the Qur'an,

• such as His saying: ﴿And when We send down upon it water, it stirs and swells. Indeed He who revived it is the reviver of the dead﴾ [Fussilat: 39];

• and His saying: ﴿And We revived thereby a dead land. Thus will be the emergence﴾ [Qaf: 11], meaning: thus will be your revival, your emergence from your graves alive after death;

• and His saying: ﴿And He brings the earth to life after its death. And thus you will be brought forth﴾ [Ar-Rum: 19], meaning: from your graves alive after death;

• and His saying: ﴿Until, when they carry heavy clouds, We drive them to a dead land and send down thereby water, and bring out thereby all kinds of fruits. Thus We bring out the dead; perhaps you will remember﴾ [Al-A'raf: 57];

• and His saying: ﴿And you see the earth barren, but when We send down upon it water, it stirs and swells and grows from every beautiful pair﴾ ﴿That is because Allah is the Truth, and He gives life to the dead, and He is competent over all things﴾ [Al-Hajj: 5, 6];

and other verses as previously mentioned.

These three proofs are very frequently used as argument for resurrection in the Book of Allah, as you have seen and as has preceded.

There is a fourth proof, also frequently used as argument for resurrection, and no mention of it is in these verses: Allah's giving life to some of the dead in this worldly abode, as the indication to it has previously been mentioned in "Surat al-Baqarah"; because He who revives one soul after its death is able to revive all souls: ﴿Your creation and your resurrection are not but as one soul﴾ [Luqman: 28].

Allah, exalted and majestic is He, has mentioned this proof in "Surat al-Baqarah" in five places:

The first is His saying: ﴿Then We revived you after your death, perhaps you would be grateful﴾ [Al-Baqarah: 56].

The second is His saying: ﴿So We said, "Strike it with part of it." Thus Allah brings the dead to life and shows you His signs; perhaps you will understand﴾ [Al-Baqarah: 73].

The third is His saying, exalted and majestic is He: ﴿Allah said to them, "Die," and then He revived them﴾ [Al-Baqarah: 243].

The fourth is His saying: ﴿So Allah caused him to die for a hundred years and then revived him. He said, "How long have you remained?" He said, "I have remained a day or part of a day." He said, "Rather, you have remained a hundred years. But look at your food and your drink; they have not spoiled. And look at your donkey—and that We may make you a sign for the people. And look at the bones, how We raise them and then clothe them with flesh." And when it became clear to him, he said, "I know that Allah is competent over all things"﴾ [Al-Baqarah: 259].

(p-340) The fifth is His saying: ﴿He said, "Take four birds and make them incline toward you. Then place on each mountain a portion of them; then call them, and they will come to you in haste. And know that Allah is Mighty and Wise"﴾ [Al-Baqarah: 260].

As for His saying, exalted and majestic is He, in this noble verse: ﴿And from it are trees in which you pasture [your livestock]﴾ [An-Nahl: 10], meaning: you graze your grazing livestock in those trees which are the pasture. The Arabs apply the term "shajar" (trees) to everything the earth produces of pasture; from this is the saying of an-Namir ibn Tawlab al-Ukli:

We came to you when the journey had been long, leading lean horses in which there is a limp;
We feed them meat when the trees (pasture) are scarce.

The Arabs say: "The livestock grazed" (sāmat al-mawāshi), when they pastured in the pasture which Allah causes to grow by rain. And "its owner pastured it" (asāmahā ṣāḥibuhā), i.e., he pastured it therein. From this is the poet's saying:

Like the son of Bazi'a, or like another similar to him,
Woe to you, son of the one who pastures the camels!

meaning: O son of the woman who pastures the camels which you graze in the pasture.

As for His saying: ﴿He causes to grow for you thereby the crops﴾ [An-Nahl: 11], Shu'bah related from 'Asim: "nunbitu" with the nun, and the rest read with the yā' (ya) at the beginning.

Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 16, verse 12:

His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿And He has subjected to you the night and the day, and the sun and the moon, and the stars are subjected by His command. Indeed in that are signs for a people who use their intellect.﴾ In this noble verse, He - exalted and high - mentioned that He subjected to His creation five great things, in which is of His great blessing what none knows but Him, and in which are the clear proofs for people of intellect that He is the One deserving to be worshipped alone.

The five mentioned are: the night, the day, the sun, the moon, and the stars.

The Qur'an repeats the mention of His bestowal through subjecting these things, and that they are among the greatest proofs of His oneness and His deserving to be worshipped alone;

- such as His saying, exalted is He: ﴿Indeed your Lord is Allah, who created the heavens and the earth in six days; then He established Himself above the Throne. He causes the night to cover the day, pursuing it rapidly, and [He created] the sun and the moon and the stars, subjected by His command. Unquestionably, His is the creation and the command. Blessed is Allah, Lord of the worlds.﴾ [Al-A'raf: 54]. His causing the night to cover the day is their subjection; and His saying: ﴿And He has subjected to you the sun and the moon, both continuing [in their courses], and He has subjected to you the night and the day﴾ [Ibrahim: 33] - the verse;

- and His saying: ﴿And a sign for them is the night; We remove from it the day, and thereupon they are in darkness. And the sun runs on to its stationary point. That is the decree of the Exalted in Might, the Knowing. And the moon - We have determined for it phases until it returns like the old dried curved stalk of a palm.﴾ [Ya-Sin: 37-39] (p-341)

- and His saying: ﴿And We have certainly adorned the nearest heaven with lamps and made them as missiles against the devils﴾ - the verse [Al-Mulk: 5];

- and His saying: ﴿And by the star they are guided﴾ [An-Nahl: 16];

and other verses besides these.

In this noble verse there are three Sab'i readings (readings of the seven canonical readers) concerning the last four nouns, which are: "ash-shams" (the sun), "al-qamar" (the moon), "an-nujum" (the stars), and "musakhkharat" (subjected) [An-Nahl: 12]. Nafi', Ibn Kathir, Abu 'Amr, Hamzah, al-Kisa'i, and 'Asim in the narration of Shu'bah read all of them in the accusative. Ibn 'Amir read the four nouns in the nominative, so that "wash-shams" is a subject (mubtada') and what follows it is conjoined to it, and "musakhkharat" is the predicate of the subject. Hafs, on the authority of 'Asim, read "wash-shams" and "al-qamar" in the accusative as conjoined to "al-layl" and "an-nahar", and read "an-nujum" in the nominative with "musakhkharat" as a subject and predicate. The most apparent grammatical analysis of "musakhkharat" according to the accusative reading is that it is a emphasizing circumstantial clause (hal mu'akkidah) for its governing element. Taskhir (subjection) in the language means: making submissive (tadhlil).

Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 16, verse 37:

His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿If you are eager for their guidance, then indeed Allah does not guide those who lead astray﴾. In this verse He, glorified and exalted, mentions that the eagerness of the Prophet ﷺ for the Islam of his people does not guide those whom Allah's knowledge has already determined to be wretched.

He clarified this meaning in other places, such as:

• His saying: ﴿Indeed, you do not guide whom you like, but Allah guides whom He wills﴾ [al-Qasas: 56];

• His saying: ﴿And whomever Allah intends to test, you cannot avail him anything against Allah. Those are the ones for whom Allah does not intend to purify their hearts. For them in this world is disgrace, and for them in the Hereafter is a great punishment﴾ [al-Ma'idah: 41];

• His saying: ﴿Whomever Allah leaves astray, there is no guide for him. And He leaves them in their transgression, wandering blindly﴾ [al-A'raf: 186];

• His saying: ﴿And whomever He intends to leave astray, He makes his breast constricted and narrow, as if he were climbing into the sky﴾ [al-An'am: 125];

and other verses besides these.

Naafi', Ibn 'Aamir, Ibn Kathir and Abu 'Amr recited this letter: ﴿Indeed Allah does not guide whom He leads astray﴾ [al-Nahl: 37], with a ḍamma on the yā' and a fatḥa on the dāl, and "man" (whom) is in the passive form "yuhda" (is guided). His saying "man" is the deputy of the subject (nā'ib al-fā'il). The meaning is: whoever Allah has led astray is not guided, that is, has no guide.

ʿĀṣim, Ḥamzah and al-Kisā'ī recited it with a fatḥa on the yā' and a kasra on the dāl, "man yahdī" in the active form. His saying "man" is the direct object of "yahdī", and the subject is a pronoun referring back to Allah, exalted. The meaning is: whoever Allah has led astray, Allah does not guide him. On this reading, it refers to those for whom wretchedness has preceded in Allah's knowledge, because others may be astray and then Allah guides them, as is well known.

Some scholars said: "He does not guide whoever leads astray, as long as he is in his leading others astray. If Allah lifts this misguidance from him, then there is no obstacle to his being guided." And knowledge is with Allah, exalted.

Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 16, verse 43:

His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿And We sent not before you except men to whom We revealed⁠—﴾, in this noble verse He mentions that He did not send before him (ﷺ) any messengers except men, that is, not angels. This is because the disbelievers found it very strange that God should send messengers from among mankind, and they said: "God is too great to send a human who eats food and walks in the markets. If He truly sent anyone, He would have sent angels," as He clarified in many verses,

such as His saying: ﴿Is it a wonder for the people that We revealed to a man from among them, [saying], "Warn the people"?﴾ [Yunus: 2];

and His saying: ﴿But they wonder that a warner has come to them from among themselves...﴾ [Qaf: 2];

and His saying: ﴿And they say, "What is this messenger that eats food and walks in the markets?"﴾ [al-Furqan: 7];

and His saying: ﴿And nothing prevented the people from believing when guidance came to them except that they said, "Has God sent a human as a messenger?"﴾ [al-Isra': 94];

and His saying: ﴿That is because their messengers used to come to them with clear proofs, and they said, "Would humans guide us?" So they disbelieved and turned away, and God was free of need﴾ [al-Taghabun: 6];

and His saying: ﴿"Shall we follow a single human from among us?"⁠﻿...﴾ the verse [al-Qamar: 24];

and His saying: ﴿So the eminent ones who disbelieved among his people said, "This is not but a human like you who wants to become superior over you. And if God had willed, He would have sent down angels. We have not heard of this among our forefathers of old"﴾ [al-Mu'minun: 24];

and His saying: ﴿And the eminent ones among his people who disbelieved and denied the meeting of the Hereafter, and whom We had given luxury in the worldly life, said, "This is not but a human like you, who eats from what you eat and drinks from what you drink. And if you obey a human like yourselves, indeed you would then be losers"﴾ [al-Mu'minun: 33–34];

and His saying: ﴿They said, "You are not but humans like us who want to avert us from what our fathers worshipped"⁠﻿...﴾ the verse [Ibrahim: 10],

and other verses besides these.

God, may He be exalted, has clarified in many verses that God sent to the children of Adam only messengers from among mankind, who are men who eat food, walk in the markets, marry, and possess the other attributes of humans;

such as His saying here: ﴿And We sent not before you except men to whom We revealed. So ask the people of the reminder if you do not know﴾ [al-Nahl: 43];

and His saying: ﴿And We sent not before you except men to whom We revealed, from the people of the towns﴾;

and His saying: ﴿And We did not send before you any messengers except that they indeed ate food and walked in the markets﴾ [al-Furqan: 20];

and His saying: ﴿And We sent not before you except men to whom We revealed. So ask the people of the reminder if you do not know. And We did not make them bodies that do not eat food, nor were they immortal﴾ [al-Anbiya': 7–8];

and His saying: ﴿And We had already sent messengers before you and given them wives and offspring﴾ [al-Ra'd: 38];

and His saying: ﴿Say, "I am not an innovation among the messengers"⁠﻿...﴾ the verse [al-Ahqaf: 9],

and other verses besides these.

The majority of reciters read this letter: "yūḥā ilayhim" with the yā' of the third person and a fatha on the ḥā', in the passive voice. Ḥafs from ʿĀṣim read it: "nūḥī ilayhim" [al-Nahl: 43] with the nūn and a kasra on the ḥā', in the active voice. Likewise is His saying at the end of Surah Yusuf: ﴿except men to whom We revealed, from the people of the towns﴾ [Yusuf: 109], and at the beginning of al-Anbiya': ﴿except men to whom We revealed. So ask the people of the reminder⁠﻿...﴾ the verse [al-Anbiya': 7]. In all these places Ḥafs alone read with the nūn and a kasra on the ḥā', while the rest read with the yā' and also a fatha on the ḥā'. As for the second instance, in Surah al-Anbiya', which is His saying: ﴿And We did not send before you any messenger except that We revealed to him that there is no deity except Me, so worship Me﴾ the verse [al-Anbiya': 25].

It was read with the nūn and a kasra on the ḥā' by Hamzah, al-Kisa'i and Ḥafs. The rest read with the yā' and also a fatha on the ḥā'. Restricting the messengers to men in the mentioned verses does not contradict that there are messengers among the angels, as He, may He be exalted, said: ﴿God chooses messengers from among the angels and from among mankind﴾ [al-Hajj: 75], and He said: ﴿Praise be to God, Originator of the heavens and the earth, who made the angels messengers﴾ the verse [Fatir: 1]; because the angels are sent to the messengers, and the messengers are sent to mankind. What the disbelievers denied was the sending of messengers to mankind, and it is in this respect that God restricted the messengers to men from among mankind. Thus it does not contradict the sending of angels to the messengers with revelation, for the seizing of souls, for the subjugation of winds and clouds, for the recording of the deeds of the children of Adam, and other things; as He, may He be exalted, said: ﴿And by those who arrange an affair﴾ [al-Nazi'at: 5].

Caution.

It is understood from these verses that God never sent a woman; because of His saying: ﴿And We sent not before you except men﴾ [al-Nahl: 43]. And it is understood from His saying: ﴿So ask the people of the reminder﴾ [al-Nahl: 43] that whoever is ignorant of a ruling is obligated to ask the scholars and act upon what they give as a verdict. What is meant by "the people of the reminder" in the verse is the People of the Book; yet this nation also truly qualifies as "the people of the reminder," because of His saying: ﴿Indeed, We have sent down the Reminder⁠﻿...﴾ the verse [al-Hijr: 9]; however, what is meant in the verse is the People of the Book. The preposition in His saying: (p-380) ﴿with clear proofs and scriptures﴾ [al-Nahl: 44] — it has been said that it is connected to "We sent," falling under the rule of the exception together with "men," meaning: "And We sent none except men with clear proofs," like your saying: "I struck none except Zayd with the whip," since its original form is "I struck Zayd with the whip." It has also been said that it is connected to "men" as a qualifier of it, meaning: men clothed with clear proofs. It has also been said that it is connected to a suppressed "We sent" indicated by what precedes it, as if it were said: "With what were they sent?" It was answered: "With clear proofs." It has also been said that it is connected to "We revealed," meaning: We revealed to them with clear proofs; this was said by the author of al-Kashshaf. And knowledge is with God, may He be exalted.

Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 16, verse 86:

His saying, exalted is He: ﴿When those who associated partners with God see their partners, they will say, "Our Lord, these are our partners, whom we used to invoke besides You." But they will throw back at them the statement, "Indeed, you are liars."﴾ In this noble verse, He, majestic and exalted, mentions that on the Day of Resurrection, when the polytheists see the objects of worship they used to associate with God in His worship, they will say to their Lord: ﴿Our Lord, these are our partners, whom we used to invoke besides You﴾ [an-Nahl 16:86], and their objects of worship will deny them in that claim, so they will say to them: "You have lied! You never used to worship us" [Yunus 10:28].

He clarified this meaning in many verses, such as:

• His saying: ﴿And who is more astray than one who invokes besides God those who will not respond to him until the Day of Resurrection, while they are unaware of their invocation? And when the people are gathered, they will be enemies to them and will deny their worship﴾ [al-Ahqaf 46:5–6];

• And His saying: ﴿They have taken besides God gods, that they might be for them a source of strength. No! They will deny their worship of them and will be against them opponents﴾ [Maryam 19:81–82];

• And His saying: ﴿Then, on the Day of Resurrection, some of you will disavow others and curse one another, and your refuge will be the Fire, and you will have no helpers﴾ [al-'Ankabut 29:25];

• And His saying: ﴿And it will be said, "Call upon your partners," so they will call upon them, but they will not respond to them﴾ [al-Qasas 28:64];

• And His saying: ﴿And We will separate them, and their partners will say, "You never used to worship us"﴾ [Yunus 10:28];

and other verses besides these.

If it is said: How could their gods deny them and disown that they worshipped them, when the reality is contrary to what they said, and they used to worship them in the worldly life besides God? The answer: their denial of them is directed at their claim that they are gods, that their worship is true, and that it brings them near to God in closeness. There is no doubt that all of this is among the greatest lies and most monstrous fabrications. Therefore, they are truthful in what they said to them, and they uttered, in that, that they (the worshippers) are liars. The purpose of the disbelievers in their saying to their Lord, "These are our partners," it is said, is that they might burden their partners with the consequence of their sin.

It is also said: that they might be partners with them in the punishment, as He, exalted, said: ﴿Our Lord, these led us astray, so give them a double punishment of the Fire﴾ [al-A'raf 7:38]. He, exalted, has explicitly stated that they and what they worship besides God will all be in the Fire in His saying: ﴿Indeed, you and what you worship besides God are the fuel of Hell﴾ [al-Anbiya' 21:98], and He excluded from that the angels, Jesus and 'Uzayr by His saying: ﴿Indeed, those for whom the best reward has preceded from Us will be kept far away from it﴾ [al-Anbiya' 21:101], because they were not worshipped with their consent. Rather, even if they had obeyed them, they would have sincerely devoted worship to God alone, exalted is He.

Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 16, verse 102:

His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿Say: The Holy Spirit has brought it down from your Lord in truth﴾ and the rest of the verse. In this noble verse, Allah, glorified and exalted be He, commanded His Prophet (ﷺ) to say that this Qur'an, which they claimed was a fabrication because of Allah's substituting one verse in place of another, was sent down to him by the Holy Spirit from his Lord, glorified and exalted be He; therefore it is not something he fabricated. The Holy Spirit (Ruh al-Qudus) is Jibril (Gabriel), and its meaning is the Holy Spirit, i.e., the one pure from everything that is unbecoming.

He clarified this meaning in many verses, such as:

• His saying: ﴿Say: Whoever is an enemy to Jibril, then indeed he has brought it down upon your heart by the permission of Allah﴾ and the rest of the verse [al-Baqarah: 97];

• And His saying: ﴿And indeed it is a revelation from the Lord of the worlds. The Trustworthy Spirit has brought it down, upon your heart, that you may be of the warners, in a clear Arabic tongue﴾ [al-Shu'ara': 192-195];

• And His saying: ﴿And do not hasten with the Qur'an before its revelation is completed to you﴾ [Ta-Ha: 114];

• And His saying: ﴿Do not move your tongue with it to hasten it. Indeed, upon Us is its collection and its recitation. So when We have recited it, then follow its recitation﴾ [al-Qiyamah: 16-18].

And other verses besides these.
